            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Ok, I came up with a stateless way of rate-limiting FreeDesktop (and possibly other backends) notifications.\nAs mentioned in my previous comment, FreeDesktop notifications have an optional replaces-id property. It can be used to replace an existing notification that has already been served, assuming it has not been dismissed.\n\nI think we could concatenate the friend number, the type of message as a number, and the current timestamp, and use that as the replaces-id. This way, each notification will have a unique ID, unless it arrived at the same second as another one of the same type from the same person. We would include the type of message to ensure we don't change the category of an existing notification, as that would hinder the usefulness of categories.\n\nWhen receiving queued messages from peers, you'd end up with only one notification, the last message they sent.\nSimilarly, if someone sends messages in realtime more than once per second (which can happen naturally but not often i'd imagine), you'd overwrite the earlier messages with the last one.\nGrouping notifications like \"3 messages from 2 friends\" cannot be done statelessly.\n\nThis could easily be a setting as well, if the user's notification daemon had its own solution for excessive notifications, or the existing behavior is desired.",
